- yash-suite-other-hangs: a full sweep of all 119 `tests/yash/*.tst`
  files (each run individually with a 10s timeout via `run-tst.sh`,
  same as CTest's own per-file `TIMEOUT` but much shorter to survey
  quickly) found several files that still hang or run suspiciously
  long, so `DO_YASH_TESTS` staying off by default (see `TODO.md`) is
  correct:

    arith-y.tst, cmdprint-y.tst, pipeline-y.tst, redir-y.tst,
    until-y.tst, while-y.tst -- all hit the 10s timeout (still running,
    not just slow).

    function-y.tst (~8s), if-y.tst (~9.5s), typeset-y.tst (~8.5s) --
    finished but suspiciously slow for a conformance test file; may or
    may not be a real bug (could just be an intentionally slow test
    case), not investigated.

  None of these are isolated to a specific line/case yet -- next step:
  debug build + debugger, or bisecting each file's test cases
  individually via a copy with cases removed.

- eval-lineno-imprecise-inside-function: `$LINENO` read from within an
  `eval`'d string now counts from the invoking statement's line
  (fixed, see fixes/92) rather than always reporting 1, but the value
  it inherits (`parse_lineno`, stamped once per top-level list by
  `sh_loop()`) is the *call site's* line, not the eval statement's own
  line when eval runs from inside a function or loop body whose
  definition spans multiple lines -- there is no per-command location
  tracking to fall back on for that case (unlike a direct, non-eval'd
  `$LINENO` reference, which reads the parse-time `loc` stamped
  individually on its own `N_ARGPARAM` node). Confirmed:

    f() {
      echo $LINENO   # correctly prints 2
      eval 'echo $LINENO'   # prints 5 (f's call site), should print 3
    }
    f

- set-notify-unimplemented: POSIX `-b` (report a background job's
  completion as soon as it happens, instead of waiting until just
  before the next prompt) is missing the same way -- no `struct
  shopt` bit, not recognized by `builtin_set()`. Belongs in `src/job/`:
  `job_wait.c`/`sh_onsig()`'s SIGCHLD path and `job_clean.c` are where
  a finished background job is currently *noticed*, but
  `job_clean.c`'s own banner-printing comments already describe the
  existing race between the async SIGCHLD path and the synchronous
  `job_wait()` path around exactly this "print immediately vs. defer"
  distinction (a stray banner interrupting a running foreground
  command's own output mid-line is the whole reason it's deferred by
  default) -- so this is more than flipping a condition, it's adding
  a real "OK to interrupt terminal output right now" toggle to logic
  that currently assumes deferral is always correct.

- set-verbose-unimplemented: POSIX `-v` (echo each input line to
  stderr as it's read, before any parsing/expansion touches it --
  distinct from `-x`, which echoes *after* expansion, from
  `src/eval/`'s `E_PRINT` path) is missing the same way. There is
  currently no hook point that sees "a raw input line" as a unit
  separate from `src/source.h`'s character-at-a-time
  `source_peek()`/`source_next()` interface the parser reads through
  -- implementing this needs either a small line-buffering layer at
  that level (echo-and-flush whenever a newline is consumed) or some
  other way to reconstruct "the line just parsed" from `struct
  source`'s position tracking. This is the most structurally invasive
  of this group precisely because nothing else needs "raw source
  text" as such a unit today.

- set-histexpand-unimplemented: `struct shopt`'s `histexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-H`) is set correctly by `set -H`/`set +H` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but nothing checks it either --
  more fundamentally, there is no history-expansion ('!') feature
  implemented at all under `src/history/` or `src/term/` for this
  flag to gate. It's a placeholder with nothing behind it.

- ubsan-buffer-op-proto-function-type-mismatch: `lib/buffer.h`'s
  `buffer_op_proto` is declared as `ssize_t(int, void*, size_t,
  void*)`. `read`/`write` (external libc, `int(int,void*,size_t)`) are
  cast to `buffer_op_proto*` throughout `src/fd/`, `src/fdtable/`,
  `src/history/`, and `src/sh/` -- UB by the letter of the standard,
  but `-fsanitize=function` never flags it: it embeds its type-
  descriptor comparison only in functions the sanitizer itself
  compiled, and libc's `read`/`write` weren't. Not planned to be
  "fixed", since a fix here would mean wrapping two libc functions
  everywhere they're used for a change with no observable effect
  under any sanitizer or real ABI this project targets.

  Two more real mismatches exist, in `lib/buffer/buffer_tosa.c`
  (`strallocwrite`, `char*` where `buffer_op_proto` wants `void*`) and
  `lib/buffer/buffer_frombuf.c` (`buffer_dummyreadbuf`, `fd_t` where
  it wants `int` -- identical on every platform this project builds
  for, but not the same declared type) -- both dead code from shish's
  own perspective (`buffer_tosa()`/`buffer_frombuf()`, the functions
  that would ever install them as a callback, are never called
  anywhere under `src/`; they only exist because `lib/`'s whole-
  directory glob build compiles every libowfat source file, used or
  not). Left alone rather than "fixed" per this repo's own standing
  preference for not touching unused `lib/` code unasked.

- asan-leak-residue-not-fully-triaged: requested directly (fix ASan
  leak reports from a full `./configure` run under the same
  `-fsanitize=address,undefined` build used above). Two of the
  largest, confirmed-real leaks (`lib/stralloc/stralloc_trunc.c`,
  `src/parse/parse_simple_command.c`) and a third found later
  (`src/redir/redir_eval.c`) are fixed (`fixes/129`, `fixes/130`,
  `fixes/133`). What's left, not yet individually triaged:

  - A large, expected residue is `struct var` entries
    (`src/var/var_create.c`) and their backing storage -- the variable
    table is deliberately process-lifetime state (like any shell's),
    reclaimed by the OS at exit rather than explicitly freed, so
    LeakSanitizer necessarily reports it as "leaked" the same way it
    would for any long-lived global root; not a bug, not planned to be
    "fixed" by adding an exit-time teardown pass whose only purpose
    would be silencing the sanitizer.
  - `sh_loop()`'s own `cmd`/`p.sa` scratch stralloc's (reused, not
    freed, once per process for the same reason) are the same
    category.
  - `redir_parse.c:108`, `expand_args.c:67`, and `eval_function.c:57`
    remain not yet investigated -- reproduced several other
    redirection shapes (append, dup, self-dup, here-doc, a bare
    trailing redirect operator) hunting for a leak whose backtrace
    actually passes through `redir_parse.c` and found none, so
    whatever line `redir_parse.c:108` referred to may have been the
    same leak already fixed as `fixes/133` (line numbers had shifted
    due to `fixes/70`'s comment additions) rather than a distinct leak
    -- not confirmed either way. Good next target if picking this up
    again: reproduce `expand_args.c:67`/`eval_function.c:57` each with
    a minimal script the way `fixes/129`/`130`/`133` were isolated,
    rather than working from the full `./configure` run's noise.
  - `src/tree/tree_string.c` (via `tree_cat()`/`tree_catlist_n()`)
    leaks 36 bytes every time a plain simple command is backgrounded
    (`eval_simple_command.c:261`, building `job->command` -- the text
    `job_banner()`/`jobs` display for that job). Minimal repro:
    `sleep 0.1 & wait` leaks on every invocation, confirmed both
    before and after `fixes/135` (unrelated to that fix, which only
    changed whether the resulting banner gets *printed*, not whether
    `job->command` gets built). Not yet root-caused to a specific
    missing free.

- break-continue-inside-eval-no-op: `break`/`continue` run via `eval`
  (e.g. `eval break`) inside an enclosing loop reports "not meaningful"
  and does not break the loop, unlike bash/dash (POSIX leaves this
  case unspecified; mksh agrees with shish here, but bash/dash/yash's
  own conformance suite expect it to work -- see
  `tests/posix/break-p.tst`'s "breaking out of eval" case). Root
  cause: `builtin_eval.c` evaluates the parsed command via
  `eval_tree(&e, cmds, E_ROOT | E_LIST)`, and `eval_jump()` treats
  *any* `E_ROOT`-flagged frame it walks through as a hard boundary
  break/continue may not cross (needed so a real subshell/top-level
  script boundary blocks an escape, see `fixes/149`) -- `eval`'s own
  internal frame doesn't need that protection but is marked the same
  way. Fixing this needs `eval`'s frame to signal "don't block search,
  but do stop return/exit here" separately from a genuine subshell
  boundary, rather than reusing `E_ROOT` for both meanings.

- posix-sigcont-etc-trap-disposition-many-failures: the
  `tests/posix/sigcont1-p.tst`, `sigcont2-p.tst`, `sigcont5-p.tst`,
  `sigcont6-p.tst` (and the same four-file pattern for `sighup`,
  `sigint`, `sigquit`, `sigterm`, `sigurg` -- 24 files total) all fail
  roughly half their cases, all under the same heading shape ("SIGxxx
  is/spares shell (main/subshell/cmdsub, self/other, +i +m, initially
  ..., X -> Y)"), e.g. `sigcont1-p.tst:7004` ("SIGCONT is handled by
  shell (main, self, +i +m, initially defaulted, clear -> command)")
  expects a trap installed after a signal disposition transition to
  actually run and prints nothing. This looks like one shared root
  cause in how trap disposition is tracked/restored across
  subshells/`fork`+`exec`/interactive+monitor-mode combinations, but
  not yet root-caused to a specific line -- next step: reduce one
  failing case out of `sigcont1-p.tst:7004` to a minimal standalone
  repro (the file uses a shared harness function, not inline script,
  so isolating the exact commands it runs is the first step) and
  compare `trap -p`'s reported state against dash/bash at each step.

- param-hash-name-vs-length-ambiguity: `${#-""}` should treat `#` as
  a variable name (since `-` follows, making it a default-value
  expansion for the variable `#`), not the `${#var}` length
  operator. Confirmed: `echo ${#-""}` prints nothing (the empty
  default) in bash/dash, but `2` in shish (length of the empty
  string that `$#` evaluates to inside double quotes). Same bug for
  `${#?X}` (should be value of variable `#` with `?` suffix, prints
  `1` instead of `0`), `${#+""}`, `${#=""}`, `${#:+}`. The parser
  in `src/parse/parse_param.c` always treats `#` as the length
  operator without checking whether the character after it is a
  valid variable-name-only expansion modifier (`-`, `+`, `=`, `?`,
  `:`, or a bare `}` with no following operator). `param-p.tst:268`.

- tilde-expansion-missing-in-case-word: `case ~/foo in /home/foo)`
  fails to match because the case word is not subject to tilde
  expansion. POSIX requires it (2.9.4.2: "The word shall be
  expanded"). Confirmed: `HOME=/home; case ~/foo in /home/foo)
  echo matched;; esac` prints nothing. Same issue likely applies to
  the case pattern (`case-p.tst:62`) and to other expansion contexts
  the parser may miss. `case-p.tst:5`.

- umask-symbolic-mode-not-implemented: `umask u+r` is treated as
  octal `0` instead of parsing the symbolic mode. Confirmed: `umask
  u+r; umask` prints `0000` in shish vs `0002` in bash. The builtin
  (`src/builtin/builtin_umask.c`) only handles octal arguments.
  POSIX requires symbolic mode support (the `umask` utility
  description specifies the grammar). `umask-p.tst:69-84` (all 56
  symbolic-mode cases fail).

- test-g-p-u-not-implemented: `test -g` (set-group-ID), `test -p`
  (FIFO/named pipe), and `test -u` (set-user-ID) are not recognized
  by `builtin_test.c`'s unary-operator dispatch — they fall through
  to the default "invalid expression" error path. Confirmed: `test
  -g /tmp` prints "test: invalid expression: /tmp" and returns 2.
  POSIX requires all three (2.9.2.8). `test-p.tst:136-223` (33
  failures across these three operators).

- readonly-builtin-reassignment-not-fatal: `readonly a=1; readonly
  a=2` succeeds silently instead of killing the non-interactive
  shell. POSIX classifies `readonly` as a special built-in utility,
  so any error in its execution (including attempting to set a
  readonly variable) must cause the shell to abort when
  non-interactive. Confirmed: `readonly a=1; readonly a=2; echo
  "not reached"` prints "not reached" with exit 0 (should die
  before reaching `echo`). The builtin (`builtin_readonly.c`)
  calls `var_copys()`/`var_chflg()` which check readonly via
  `var_set()`, but the error path only prints a message and
  returns non-zero — it doesn't call `sh_exit()` for the
  special-built-in-error-must-kill-shell case. `readonly-p.tst:26`.

- unset-on-readonly-variable-silently-succeeds: `readonly a=1;
  unset a` silently removes the variable instead of rejecting the
  operation. Confirmed: `readonly a=1; unset a; echo "a=$a"` prints
  "a=" in shish vs "bash: unset: a: cannot unset: readonly
  variable" + "a=1" in bash. `builtin_unset.c` never checks the
  `V_READONLY` flag before calling `var_unset()`. POSIX requires
  unset to fail on readonly variables (2.9.1.43). `unset-p.tst:112,118`.

- cmdsub-p-tst-hangs: `cmdsub-p.tst` times out at test 139
  ("command substitution between here-document operator and body"),
  which exercises a heredoc nested inside a command substitution
  that itself appears between a heredoc operator and its body:
  `cat <<\OUTER; echoraw "$(cat <<\INNER ...")"` — the parser/lexer
  likely enters an infinite loop trying to find the inner heredoc
  delimiter while still inside the outer one's body. Not yet
  root-caused to a specific line in the parser. `cmdsub-p.tst:139`.
